---

DNS query tools and resolver configuration. dig (from bind-tools) is the
most capable; host is quick and simple.

@code bash
# ---- dig: DNS lookup utility ----

dig example.com                           # A record (default)
dig example.com AAAA                      # IPv6 address
dig example.com MX                        # mail exchange
dig example.com NS                        # nameservers
dig example.com TXT                       # TXT records
dig example.com SOA                       # start of authority
dig example.com ANY                       # all records (often blocked)
dig example.com CNAME                     # canonical name

# query specific server
dig @8.8.8.8 example.com                  # query Google DNS
dig @1.1.1.1 example.com                  # query Cloudflare

# useful options
dig +short example.com                    # terse output
dig +noall +answer example.com            # answer section only
dig +trace example.com                    # full delegation trace from root
dig +tcp example.com                      # force TCP
dig +dnssec example.com                   # request DNSSEC records

# reverse lookup
dig -x 93.184.216.34                      # PTR record

# batch queries
dig -f queryfile.txt                      # one domain per line

# AXFR zone transfer (if permitted)
dig @ns1.example.com example.com AXFR

@code bash
# ---- nslookup ----

nslookup example.com                      # basic lookup
nslookup example.com 8.8.8.8             # use specific server
nslookup -type=mx example.com             # MX records
nslookup -type=ns example.com             # NS records
nslookup -type=txt example.com            # TXT records
nslookup -type=soa example.com            # SOA record
nslookup -type=ptr 34.216.184.93.in-addr.arpa   # reverse

@code bash
# ---- host: simple DNS lookup ----

host example.com                          # A + MX + NS
host -t AAAA example.com                  # IPv6
host -t MX example.com                    # mail
host -t TXT example.com                   # TXT
host -a example.com                       # all (verbose)
host 93.184.216.34                        # reverse lookup
host -T example.com                       # use TCP
host example.com 8.8.8.8                  # specific server

@text
/etc/resolv.conf controls the system resolver. On many setups this file is
managed by dhcpcd, NetworkManager, ConnMan, or resolvconf. Editing it
directly may be overwritten.

@code bash
# ---- /etc/resolv.conf ----

# typical contents
nameserver 192.168.1.1                    # primary DNS
nameserver 8.8.8.8                        # secondary DNS
search home.lan                           # search domain
options timeout:2 attempts:3              # resolver options
options rotate                            # round-robin nameservers
options edns0                             # enable EDNS0

# check current DNS
cat /etc/resolv.conf

# prevent overwriting (not recommended long-term)
chattr +i /etc/resolv.conf                # make immutable
chattr -i /etc/resolv.conf                # remove immutable

@code bash
# ---- resolvconf: resolver config framework ----

# install openresolv (Arch/Artix)
pacman -S openresolv

# resolvconf integrates with dhcpcd, wpa_supplicant, openvpn
# configuration in /etc/resolvconf.conf

resolvconf -u                             # update resolv.conf from inputs
resolvconf -l                             # list current configurations
resolvconf -d eth0                        # delete interface entry

@text
Common public DNS servers:

@code bash
# Google
8.8.8.8       2001:4860:4860::8888
8.8.4.4       2001:4860:4860::8844

# Cloudflare
1.1.1.1       2606:4700:4700::1111
1.0.0.1       2606:4700:4700::1001

# Quad9 (malware blocking)
9.9.9.9       2620:fe::fe

# OpenDNS
208.67.222.222
208.67.220.220

@text
Troubleshooting DNS:

@code bash
# test resolution at each level
dig +trace example.com                    # full delegation path
dig @127.0.0.1 example.com               # test local resolver
dig @192.168.1.1 example.com             # test gateway DNS

# check if DNS is the problem
ping 8.8.8.8                              # works = network ok, DNS may be bad
ping google.com                           # fails = DNS problem

# flush DNS cache (if using a local caching resolver)
# systemd-resolved (not on Artix by default)
# resolvectl flush-caches

# dnsmasq
killall -HUP dnsmasq
